+# Contract: `logredact` command-line interface
+
+**Feature**: 001-log-data-redaction-cli | **Date**: 2026-11-21
+
+The CLI is the only interface this feature exposes. This document is the authoritative statement of
+its surface; recognition rules live in [redaction-rules.md](./redaction-rules.md).
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on the module search path (FR-001), e.g.:
+
+```bash
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact --count app.log
+```
+
+**Package location**: `src/logredact/`. **Interpreter**: CPython 3.11 or newer. **Runtime imports**:
+Python standard library only — no third-party package (FR-015, Constitution Principle I).
+
+---
+
+## Arguments
+
+| Argument | Form | Cardinality | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `--count` | option, no value | 0 or 1 | also write a tally to stderr |
+| `FILE` | positional | 0..n | input path, consumed in the order given |
+
+**Rules**
+
+- `--count` is accepted in **any position** among the arguments (FR-012):
+  `--count a.log`, `a.log --count`, and `a.log --count b.log` are equivalent.
+- Every non-option argument is a FILE path, in order (FR-012). Repetition is allowed and the file is
+  read once per occurrence (US2-4).
+- A FILE of `-` is a **literal relative path**, not a stdin alias. It is opened like any other path
+  and normally fails per the error contract below.
+- Paths are used verbatim: not resolved, not normalised, not restricted to a base directory
+  (SR-009). The tool never opens any path for writing (SR-008).
+- `-h` / `--help` prints usage to **stdout** and exits **0**. It is the only flag other than
+  `--count`, and neither affects which categories are redacted (SR-003).
+- There is no option, environment variable, or configuration file that disables or weakens any
+  redaction category (SR-003, spec Assumptions).
+
+---
+
+## Input
+
+| Condition | Source read |
+|---|---|
+| no FILE arguments | standard input |
+| one or more FILE arguments | each FILE in argument order; **stdin is not read at all** (FR-002, US2-5) |
+
+- Encoding: UTF-8 with `errors="surrogateescape"`. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 pass through to
+  stdout **unchanged** (FR-017).
+- Newlines are not translated. `\n`, `\r\n`, and lone `\r` are preserved exactly (FR-003).
+- Reading is streaming and bounded: at most **1 MiB (1 048 576 characters)** of decoded content per
+  line is held at once, so total input size is unbounded (FR-016, SR-005).
+- Files are opened read-only and closed before the next is opened.
+
+---
+
+## Standard output
+
+- Every input line is written, in input order, with redactions applied. All non-matching characters
+  and the original line structure are preserved exactly (FR-003, SC-003).
+- **No trailing newline is added** to input whose final line lacked one (US1-6).
+- An empty input produces empty stdout (US2-3, edge: empty input).
+- The summary line never appears on stdout (FR-010, US3-4, SC-005).
+- Output is flushed before the process reports success.
+
+---
+
+## Standard error
+
+| Run | stderr content |
+|---|---|
+| success, no `--count` | **empty** (FR-011) |
+| success, `--count` | exactly one line: `emails=N ips=N cards=N\n` |
+| failure | one diagnostic line (see below); **no summary** (US4-5) |
+
+### Summary format (`--count` only)
+
+```text
+emails=N ips=N cards=N
+```
+
+- Exactly that form: lowercase keys, single `=`, single spaces, terminated by `\n`, no leading or
+  trailing padding.
+- Each `N` is the **total number of replacements** of that category across all input — not lines
+  affected, not distinct values. The same email twice counts twice (FR-010, US3-3).
+- Written **after** all redacted output has been written and flushed, and only on a successful run
+  (FR-010, US3-4, US4-5). With no redactable input: `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0` (US3-2).
+
+### Diagnostics
+
+One line, written to stderr, naming the problem. Diagnostics **must not** contain any input line
+content, matched value, or fragment of one; the only input-derived string permitted is a FILE path
+supplied by the invoking user (SR-001). A stack trace is never printed (SR-009).
+
+| Condition | Message shape | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| FILE does not exist | names the path, reports it cannot be read | 2 |
+| FILE permission denied | names the path, reports it cannot be read | 2 |
+| FILE is a directory | names the path, reports it cannot be read | 2 |
+| line exceeds 1 MiB | names the input source, states the limit, **does not quote the line** | 2 |
+| stdout write/flush fails | reports the write failure | 2 |
+| unrecognised option | `argparse` usage message | 2 |
+
+---
+
+## Exit status
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| **0** | all input was read, and all output was written and flushed successfully |
+| **2** | any failure: unreadable FILE, over-long line, output write failure, invalid usage |
+
+Exit status 0 is produced **only** when the full redacted stream reached stdout; a truncated output
+is never reported as success (FR-014, SR-007, INV-6, SC-006).
+
+---
+
+## Ordering and failure semantics
+
+- Files are processed in argument order and streamed as they are read; output for file *n* is
+  written before file *n+1* is opened (US2-1, US2-2).
+- Files are **not** pre-validated. An unreadable file is reported the moment it is reached, so
+  output already written to stdout is kept, and processing stops there — later files are not opened
+  (US4-4, spec Assumptions "Failure timing").
+
+---
+
+## Guarantees
+
+| ID | Guarantee |
+|---|---|
+| G-1 | Input is inert data: never evaluated, deserialised, interpolated, or executed; no shell or subprocess is invoked (SR-004) |
+| G-2 | Placeholders are inserted literally; template or backreference syntax in the input cannot alter a substitution (SR-004) |
+| G-3 | No file, temporary file, or cache is created, written, or modified; input files are opened read-only (SR-008) |
+| G-4 | No network access of any kind is performed (spec OWASP table, A10) |
+| G-5 | Matching is linear in line length; no nested unbounded repetition exists in any pattern (SR-006) |
+| G-6 | A whole recognised value is replaced, never a prefix or suffix (SR-002) |
+
+---
+
+## Internal entry point (for tests)
+
+Not part of the user-facing contract, but fixed here so tests can bind to it:
+
+```python
+def main(argv: list[str], *, stdin=None, stdout=None, stderr=None) -> int: ...
+```
+
+Returns the exit status instead of raising `SystemExit`; streams default to the real `sys` streams
+when omitted. `__main__.py` is the thin shell that calls it and exits with its return value.
+Injectable streams are what make the write-failure path (SR-007) and the stdin path testable
+deterministically — see research.md D-008.
