+# Contract: redaction recognition rules
+
+**Feature**: 001-log-data-redaction-cli | **Date**: 2026-11-21
+
+The authoritative recognition grammar for the three redaction categories, the precedence between
+them, and the worked results every implementation must reproduce. Derived from FR-004 – FR-009 and
+SR-002/SR-006; the rationale for each choice is in [research.md](../research.md).
+
+All character classes are **ASCII only**. `\d`, `\w`, and `\b` are not used anywhere: on `str`
+patterns `\d` also matches non-ASCII decimal digits, which would feed non-ASCII text to the Luhn
+check (research.md D-004).
+
+---
+
+## 1. Email → `[EMAIL]`  (rank 1, FR-004)
+
+```text
+(?<![A-Za-z0-9._%+-])
+[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]{1,256}                 # local part
+@
+(?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{1,63}\.){1,16}          # one or more dot-terminated labels
+[A-Za-z]{2,63}                          # final label: letters only, >= 2
+(?![A-Za-z0-9-])
+```
+
+| Element | Rule |
+|---|---|
+| local part | 1+ characters from letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `%`, `+`, `-` |
+| separator | a single `@` |
+| domain | 1+ dot-separated labels of letters, digits, `-` |
+| final label | at least two characters, **letters only** |
+
+**Boundaries** (FR-004, "must not be extended into, or truncated by, an adjacent character that is
+itself valid in a local part or a domain label"): the leading lookbehind rejects a start whose
+preceding character is itself local-part-valid; the trailing lookahead rejects an end followed by a
+label-valid character. A following `.` is permitted — it is a label *separator*, not a label
+character — so a sentence-final address is still recognised.
+
+**Consequences**
+
+| Input | Result | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `jane.doe+test@mail.example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | multi-label domain, `+` and `.` in local part |
+| `a@b.io` | `[EMAIL]` | final label `io` is two letters |
+| `a@1.2.3.4` | `a@[IP]` | final label `4` is not alphabetic → no email; IPv4 then applies |
+| `jane@example.com.` | `[EMAIL].` | trailing dot is a sentence boundary |
+| `a@b.co1` | unchanged | final label `co1` is not letters-only, and truncating to `a@b.co` is forbidden |
+| 300-char local part + `@b.io` | unchanged | exceeds the 256 bound; **no partial match** is emitted (SR-002) |
+
+---
+
+## 2. IPv4 → `[IP]`  (rank 2, FR-005)
+
+```text
+(?<![0-9.])
+OCTET (?:\. OCTET){3}
+(?![0-9.])
+
+OCTET := (?:25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|[01][0-9][0-9]|[0-9][0-9]|[0-9])
+```
+
+| Element | Rule |
+|---|---|
+| groups | exactly four, dot-separated |
+| octet | 1–3 digits, numeric value 0–255 |
+| leading zeros | permitted — the rule is the numeric value (`010` = 10) |
+
+**Boundaries** (FR-005): a candidate immediately preceded or followed by a digit or `.` is not
+redacted. Encoded as lookarounds so the engine never produces such a match in the first place, which
+also means a rejected candidate does not consume text and hide a later match.
+
+**Consequences**
+
+| Input | Result | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `10.0.0.7` | `[IP]` | all octets in range |
+| `010.0.0.7` | `[IP]` | leading zero permitted, value 10 |
+| `999.1.1.1` | unchanged | `999` is not an octet; the interior candidate `99.1.1.1` is digit-preceded |
+| `1.2.3.4.5` | unchanged | candidate is dot-bounded on one side |
+| `256.1.1.1` | unchanged | `256` exceeds 255; interior candidates are digit-preceded |
+| `a@1.2.3.4` | `a@[IP]` | `@` is neither a digit nor a `.` |
+
+---
+
+## 3. Payment card → `[CARD]`  (rank 3, FR-006, FR-007)
+
+Not expressible as a regular expression — the Luhn checksum requires host-language validation. The
+algorithm, applied only to text not already claimed by a rank-1 or rank-2 match:
+
+1. Find **maximal** ASCII digit runs with `[0-9]+`. Maximal runs are essential: a candidate may only
+   begin at a run start and end at a run end, which is precisely FR-007's "not immediately preceded
+   or followed by another digit".
+2. Two consecutive runs are **chained** when exactly one character separates them and it is `' '` or
+   `'-'`. Separator kinds may be mixed within one number; a double separator does not chain.
+3. For each run *i*, left to right, walk forward over chained runs *j* accumulating the digit total,
+   stopping as soon as the total exceeds 19. Record the **largest** *j* whose total is 13–19 digits
+   and whose concatenated digits pass Luhn.
+4. If a candidate was recorded, emit a match from the start of run *i* to the end of run *j*, then
+   resume at run *j+1* (non-overlapping, FR-009). Otherwise advance to run *i+1*.
+
+| Element | Rule |
+|---|---|
+| digit count | 13–19 inclusive, counting digits only |
+| grouping | contiguous, or groups of 1+ digits joined by **single** `' '` or `'-'` |
+| separators | never at the start or end of the match; kinds may be mixed |
+| checksum | Luhn over the concatenated digits must pass |
+| boundaries | the character before the match and after it must not be a digit |
+| selection | leftmost start wins; at one start, the longest valid candidate wins |
+
+**Luhn**: right to left over the digits, double every second digit, subtract 9 from any doubled
+value above 9, sum all; valid when the sum mod 10 is 0.
+
+**Consequences**
+
+| Input | Result | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `4111 1111 1111 1111` | `[CARD]` | 16 digits, single-space groups, Luhn passes |
+| `4111-1111 1111-1111` | `[CARD]` | mixed single separators permitted |
+| `4111  1111 1111 1111` | unchanged | double separator breaks the chain; no part reaches 13 digits |
+| `4111 1111 1111 1111 1234` | `[CARD] 1234` | the 16-digit candidate ends at a run end; 20 digits exceeds the maximum |
+| `1234567890123` | unchanged | 13 digits but Luhn fails |
+| `x4111111111111111` | `x[CARD]` | preceding `x` is not a digit |
+| `edge4111111111111111edge` | `edge[CARD]edge` | letters do not block the match |
+| 20+ contiguous digits | unchanged | no 13–19 digit candidate ends at a run end |
+| `١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩٠١٢٣` | unchanged | non-ASCII digits are not digits here |
+
+---
+
+## 4. Precedence and overlap  (FR-008, FR-009)
+
+Fixed precedence: **email (1) → IPv4 (2) → card (3)**.
+
+1. The email and IPv4 alternatives are scanned as **one** alternation in that order, non-overlapping
+   and left to right. At a shared start position the email alternative wins because it is listed
+   first.
+2. Card detection runs **only in the gaps** left uncovered by step 1. A higher-ranked match
+   therefore suppresses any overlapping card candidate — including a card candidate that starts
+   earlier. Precedence is applied before position.
+3. Within one category: leftmost wins; at one start position the longest valid candidate wins.
+4. The output line is rebuilt by concatenating verbatim slices with **literal** placeholder strings.
+   Placeholders are never nested, never doubly substituted, and **never re-scanned** (FR-008).
+   Substitution uses no replacement template, so `\1`, `\g<0>`, or the text `[EMAIL]` appearing in
+   the input cannot alter a replacement (SR-004).
+5. Each replacement increments exactly one counter (FR-010).
+
+| Input | Result | Rule |
+|---|---|---|
+| `12345678903@example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | email outranks card at the same start |
+| `4111111111111111@a.com` | `[EMAIL]` | email outranks card at the same start |
+| `a@1.2.3.4` | `a@[IP]` | not an email; IPv4 applies |
+| `12 34.5.6.7` | `12 [IP]` | the IPv4 match is not blocked by a failed card candidate |
+| `12 34@x.com` | `12 [EMAIL]` | likewise for email |
+| `a@b.io 1.2.3.4` | `[EMAIL] [IP]` | multiple matches, both counted |
+
+---
+
+## 5. Complexity guarantee  (SR-006)
+
+- No pattern contains nested unbounded repetition, so catastrophic backtracking is structurally
+  impossible rather than merely unobserved.
+- All quantifiers are explicitly bounded except `[0-9]+`, a single non-nested repetition of a
+  one-character class that the engine consumes in one linear scan with no backtracking states.
+  Bounding it would introduce false digit-run boundaries and break FR-007 (research.md D-005).
+- Card candidate enumeration is bounded: each start position examines at most 19 runs, because the
+  walk stops once the digit total exceeds 19.
+- Measured worst case on 10 000-character adversarial lines: **117 ms** (a separator run), against
+  SC-008's 1 s budget.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Placeholders
+
+| Category | Placeholder | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| email | `[EMAIL]` | exact literal, no padding or counter |
+| IPv4 | `[IP]` | exact literal |
+| card | `[CARD]` | exact literal |
+
+Placeholders are fixed and non-reversible: no length hint, hash, or partial value is emitted, so no
+input value is recoverable from the output (SR-002, Security Baseline "Output, log, and error
+hygiene").
